WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.254 --> 00:00:05.316
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.316 --> 00:00:11.935
19 of the last 21 years have been
the hottest years on record.

00:00:11.935 --> 00:00:16.035
Planet Earth has all ready
warmed by 1.1 degrees

00:00:16.035 --> 00:00:20.526
Celsius from the planet on
which homo sapiens evolved.

00:00:20.526 --> 00:00:23.047
Climate change catastrophe is coming.

00:00:26.566 --> 00:00:29.033
We got through it.

00:00:29.033 --> 00:00:33.105
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Have I managed to put any of you to

00:00:33.105 --> 00:00:34.338
sleep yet?

00:00:34.338 --> 00:00:39.036
Yawn, I'm bored already with this speech.

00:00:39.036 --> 00:00:44.951
And it's okay,
you can admit it, so are you.

00:00:44.951 --> 00:00:47.658
So let's reset.

00:00:47.658 --> 00:00:52.074
Communication about climate change sucks,

00:00:52.074 --> 00:00:57.544
it all sounds the same and
everybody wants to be Al Gore.

00:00:57.544 --> 00:01:01.199
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But in trying to scare people into

00:01:01.199 --> 00:01:06.266
taking action, I believe we miss
the opportunity to make the basic

00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:12.164
point that there is no better career
than working to address climate change.

00:01:12.164 --> 00:01:14.951
It checks all the boxes.

00:01:14.951 --> 00:01:17.766
You hope to build financial security?

00:01:17.766 --> 00:01:19.983
Work in climate.

00:01:19.983 --> 00:01:23.390
In the back,
you hope to work at the tip of the spear,

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:27.584
solve interesting problems
with real-world ramifications?

00:01:27.584 --> 00:01:29.480
Work in climate.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:33.152
And you hope to find
meaning in your work and

00:01:33.152 --> 00:01:37.846
to be proud of your contributions
to the greater good?

00:01:37.846 --> 00:01:40.266
Work in climate.

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:45.905
This is not the climate change talk
you've heard 100 times before.

00:01:45.905 --> 00:01:51.344
I'm not here to scare you,
I'm here to recruit.

00:01:51.344 --> 00:01:56.518
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It is

00:01:56.518 --> 00:02:02.110
tragic that climate change exists,
of course.

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:07.126
However, for an individual
strategically shaping their career,

00:02:07.126 --> 00:02:12.142
climate change represents a once
in a lifetime opportunity to skate

00:02:12.142 --> 00:02:17.862
where the puck is going on trillion
dollar industries with massive policy and

00:02:17.862 --> 00:02:19.723
regulatory tailwinds.

00:02:19.723 --> 00:02:24.880
Global scope, but that are somehow
still young enough to have low

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:30.615
barriers to leadership despite enormous,
almost guaranteed growth.

00:02:30.615 --> 00:02:36.515
And at the same time, careers and
climate are an opportunity to pursue

00:02:36.515 --> 00:02:41.718
work whose impact will reverberate for
generations to come.

00:02:41.718 --> 00:02:49.125
An opportunity to do well for yourself and
to do good for those around you.

00:02:51.796 --> 00:02:56.178
So for the next six minutes,
doing well and

00:02:56.178 --> 00:03:00.578
doing good is all I'm going to talk about.

00:03:00.578 --> 00:03:04.210
First, doing well.

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:08.791
Climate change is
fundamentally different from

00:03:08.791 --> 00:03:12.924
any of the economic
megatrends of the past,

00:03:12.924 --> 00:03:17.281
the steam engine, the wheel, electricity,

00:03:17.281 --> 00:03:21.878
steel, the Internet,
please no one say Web 3.

00:03:21.878 --> 00:03:28.219
Each of these things happened, and
they were important steps forward for

00:03:28.219 --> 00:03:33.756
our species, but each of these
things just happened to happen.

00:03:33.756 --> 00:03:38.652
Facebook exists, but it wasn't inevitable
that Mark Zuckerberg would create it.

00:03:38.652 --> 00:03:43.443
Climate change is not
like these other things,

00:03:43.443 --> 00:03:49.931
because facing climate change
is mathematically inevitable.

00:03:49.931 --> 00:03:56.932
Either we will beat it or it will beat us.

00:03:56.932 --> 00:04:00.746
And inevitability means opportunity.

00:04:00.746 --> 00:04:05.085
Think about how many times in history
have you been able to look 30 years into

00:04:05.085 --> 00:04:09.507
the future and know with very little
uncertainty which industries will grow?

00:04:09.507 --> 00:04:12.900
The logic is extremely simple,

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:18.304
either we lose the fight
against climate change and

00:04:18.304 --> 00:04:22.828
the economic wheel stops turning, fire,

00:04:22.828 --> 00:04:26.867
famine, flood, also other F words.

00:04:26.867 --> 00:04:29.365
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Either we lose or

00:04:29.365 --> 00:04:33.040
if we're going to win and
secure a future for our species,

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:36.575
we know pretty much which
industries will need to grow.

00:04:36.575 --> 00:04:39.980
Electrify everything and
power it with renewables, right?

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:46.840
Renewable energy, energy storage,
regenerative agriculture,

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:51.543
decarbonize transport,
I could go on and on.

00:04:51.543 --> 00:04:57.361
The nascency of these industries also
means low barriers to leadership for

00:04:57.361 --> 00:04:59.493
those who want to get ahead.

00:04:59.493 --> 00:05:04.798
Think of any investment bank,
odds are there's a managing director

00:05:04.798 --> 00:05:09.742
there who's been doing some
version of that job for 50 years.

00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:12.730
How can you or I compete?

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:20.086
On the other hand, the electric bus
industry has existed for five years.

00:05:20.086 --> 00:05:25.028
Which means for any young,
smart, ambitious person,

00:05:25.028 --> 00:05:30.070
like any of you in this room,
you have an opportunity to be

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:37.343
among the best in the world in very little
time because the playing field is level.

00:05:39.115 --> 00:05:43.447
Opportunity abounds for journalists,

00:05:43.447 --> 00:05:48.167
geologists, policymakers, investors.

00:05:48.167 --> 00:05:52.679
Yes, for lifeguards, because chances
are we're going to have a lot more water.

00:05:52.679 --> 00:05:56.406
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Absolutely everyone has something to

00:05:56.406 --> 00:05:58.940
gain from participating
in the new economy.

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:05.205
And the earlier you get in,
the more valuable you'll be.

00:06:05.205 --> 00:06:09.201
If you want to do well, work in climate.

00:06:12.344 --> 00:06:18.610
But isn't life about more than
just doing well financially?

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:23.540
There's an entire other set of criteria,
I believe,

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:29.540
we're all hoping to find at work to
make the world a better place and

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:34.173
pursue our passions,
to help others, to do good.

00:06:37.766 --> 00:06:42.469
I spent the years before Stanford
as a young executive building

00:06:42.469 --> 00:06:46.028
a solar microgrid company
in Southern Africa.

00:06:46.028 --> 00:06:52.511
Picture an urban Zambian slum called
[FOREIGN] during rainy season.

00:06:54.654 --> 00:06:58.194
Tin roofs thrumming with falling rain,

00:06:58.194 --> 00:07:02.158
turgid rivers of mud
flowing in the streets.

00:07:04.639 --> 00:07:12.218
My company had recently installed a solar
microgrid here, this solar microgrid.

00:07:12.218 --> 00:07:17.054
So one rainy day,
I was leading a site visit and

00:07:17.054 --> 00:07:24.867
escaped the rain into someone's home
as the sun set and darkness settled.

00:07:24.867 --> 00:07:30.220
This was about the time of the day
when at home here at Stanford,

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:32.950
I would just turn on the lights.

00:07:32.950 --> 00:07:37.705
But these people couldn't do that,
so it just got dark.

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:47.610
We sat, We waited,

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:53.010
and then light, warm light flooded

00:07:53.010 --> 00:07:57.699
the home, energizing us all.

00:07:57.699 --> 00:08:02.532
These people, this family had light and

00:08:02.532 --> 00:08:07.658
power and
a better chance at a better life.

00:08:07.658 --> 00:08:11.050
I can't pretend to know true poverty,
I was just a visitor.

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:15.881
But for a few moments,
I experienced firsthand what 800

00:08:15.881 --> 00:08:20.142
million people without
electricity live through.

00:08:20.142 --> 00:08:25.526
For me,
this was truly a light bulb moment.

00:08:25.526 --> 00:08:31.154
It was a light bulb moment
because when I saw the impact

00:08:31.154 --> 00:08:37.294
that my work had on real people,
I felt a sense of purpose.

00:08:37.294 --> 00:08:40.166
I felt that my work had meaning.

00:08:42.312 --> 00:08:45.251
I felt that my life had meaning.

00:08:45.251 --> 00:08:50.575
It's such a gift to truly
believe in what you do.

00:08:50.575 --> 00:08:55.262
And I believe strongly that nothing
merits such belief more than

00:08:55.262 --> 00:08:59.430
preventing human suffering
induced by climate change,

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:04.403
and facing up to the greatest
challenge our species has ever known.

00:09:04.403 --> 00:09:10.901
If you want to do good, Work in climate.

00:09:10.901 --> 00:09:15.805
But at the end of the day,
the beauty of it is you do

00:09:15.805 --> 00:09:19.872
not have to choose between doing well and

00:09:19.872 --> 00:09:24.310
doing good if you choose
to work in climate.

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:28.339
I'm absolutely convinced
it's the best of both.

00:09:28.339 --> 00:09:32.195
I'm inspired by the monumental
opportunity to shape

00:09:32.195 --> 00:09:34.858
the next thousand years of history.

00:09:34.858 --> 00:09:40.358
And I'm damn sure that we
need every last one of us.

00:09:40.358 --> 00:09:47.746
So, my friends, I invite you,
work in climate, thank you.

00:09:47.746 --> 00:09:49.039
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:49.039 --> 00:09:53.780
[MUSIC]

